Now upgrade PosterCard. Requirements: rating chip, year/title over gradient overlay on hover; optional progress prop shows bottom progress bar + %. Keep placeholder & skeleton classes.

Careful: `.poster-card.skeleton` is used separately for skeleton elements (SectionRail renders skeleton as standalone divs, not PosterCard). Search results grid in HomePage uses PosterCard within `.poster-grid`. Existing grid uses `.poster-link` wrapper buttons. Keep same API.

New PosterCard layout:
```
<article class="poster-card">
  image or placeholder letter
  <div class="poster-card-overlay">
    <span class="poster-card-rating">★ 8.4</span> (top-right chip maybe) 
  </div>
  <div class="poster-card-info"> title, year </div>
  optional progress bar:
  {progress != null && <div class="poster-card-progress"><div style width/></div>}
</article>
```

Rating chip only when voteAverage>0. Overlay appears on hover (opacity 0→1). Also want always-visible year. Keep bottom info bar.

Let me design visually: poster image full. Bottom gradient overlay always shows title & year for legibility (instead of solid panel below). Actually existing shows info under image in a panel. For a "cinematic" look, overlay on image bottom is nicer. But DownloadPanel thumbnails etc unaffected (they use own class).

Since grid width from parent; images keep aspect 2/3.

Implement progress prop optional number 0..1. PosterCard also used by home rails and search grid & results grid. Provide optional `progress` and also optional `onClick`? Click handled by wrapper button in pages. But HeroSpotlight separate.

Let me implement PosterCard:

```tsx
import type { MediaItem } from '../types';
import { posterUrl } from '../api';

interface PosterCardProps {
  item: MediaItem;
  progress?: number | null;
}

export function PosterCard({ item, progress }: PosterCardProps) {
  const poster = posterUrl(item.posterPath);
  const pct = progress == null ? null : Math.min(100, Math.max(0, Math.round(progress * 100)));
  const showRating = item.voteAverage > 0;
  return (
    <article className="poster-card">
      {poster ? (
        <img className="poster-card-image" src={poster} alt={`${item.title} poster`} loading="lazy" />
      ) : (
        <div className="poster-card-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
          {item.title.charAt(0).toUpperCase()}
        </div>
      )}
      {showRating && (
        <span className="poster-card-rating" title={`Rating ${item.voteAverage.toFixed(1)}`}>
          ★ {item.voteAverage.toFixed(1)}
        </span>
      )}
      <div className="poster-card-info">
        <h3 className="poster-card-title" title={item.title}>{item.title}</h3>
        <span className="poster-card-year">{item.year ?? '—'}</span>
      </div>
      {pct != null && (
        <div className="poster-card-progress" aria-label={`${pct}% downloaded`}>
          <div className="poster-card-progress-fill" style={{ width: `${pct}%` }} />
          <span className="poster-card-progress-label">{pct}%</span>
        </div>
      )}
    </article>
  );
}
```

The rating chip to be positioned top-right over image. poster-card-info at bottom overlay. For grid layout, we need info overlay inside image area not extending height. Actually overlay requires image occupy whole card; that means title/rating overlay onto poster. With the info overlay covering bottom ~ 2 lines plus for progress. Card height equals image height. Use position:relative card; image block. Add `.poster-card-shade` gradient at bottom always, and info absolutely at bottom over it.

But in skeleton rows, `.poster-card.skeleton` separate elements so no issue.

Loading="lazy" fine.

Let me finalize. I'll place overlay shade + info. The `.poster-card` must not have extra height beyond image; grid cell equals image; overlay absolute bottom. Let's set card position relative; image width 100% aspect ratio; info absolute inset-x bottom padding 10px 12px; gradient. progress bar absolute bottom above info? If both, info may overlap. For in-progress torrents there's no requirement that title overlay shows - we can put progress at the very bottom below the image? Simplify: progress bar overlays at bottom of image (thin). Info overlay sits slightly above progress when progress present? Complexity.

