`download` object identity changes each snapshot (new array each 2s) — the effect depends on `download`, might re-run each poll, re-recording. Upsert moves to front each 2s while on watch page → fine, but repeated setState each 2s is wasteful. Could depend on infoHash/tmdbId primitives instead. Let me depend on specific fields to avoid re-run on each poll. Simplest: compute key fields, dependency array [download?.infoHash, download?.tmdbId]. Actually the effect closure references `download`. Better depend on stable scalars. Since `download` may be found late (store empty initially), once found we record once. Use primitive deps:

```
const tmdbId = download?.tmdbId ?? null; const mediaType = download?.mediaType ?? null;
useEffect(() => { if (!download || !tmdbId || !mediaType) return; recordRecent({...from download}); }, [infoHash, tmdbId, mediaType, recordRecent]);
```

But eslint may require download dep; but I reference download inside. React hooks exhaustive-deps wants all referenced values. To keep clean: recompute inside effect from downloads? Simpler store the record fields as consts derived then use them inside effect without referencing download. Let me restructure:

```
const active = downloads.find(...)
useEffect(() => {
  const d = active;
  if (!d || !d.tmdbId || !d.mediaType) return;
  recordRecent({...})
}, [active?.infoHash, active?.tmdbId, active?.mediaType, active?.title, active?.torrentName, active?.year, active?.posterPath, recordRecent]);
```

That's ugly but satisfies lint & stable-ish (title/year/poster change rarely). Actually simpler: use optional chaining primitives in deps and reference `download` inside but add eslint disable? Prefer not. Another approach: subscribe to store with selector returning needed slice as primitive snapshot string keyed by infoHash? Too much.

Since recordRecent upsert each 2s is idempotent-ish but writes localStorage & setState each time. HomePage recents rail subscribes → re-render every 2s while watching page open (but WatchPage is separate route; HomePage unmounted then, so fine). localStorage writes 1/2s modest but okay. Still better to avoid.

I'll restructure with explicit primitive deps list referencing properties not object. That handles lint? exhaustive-deps may still complain that `download` used but not listed. Using variables like `d?.tmdbId` inside effect means referencing d; deps must include d too technically. Hmm.

Easier: pull the needed values out before effect as consts; effect uses those consts and records them. Then deps = [infoHash, tmdbId, mediaType, title, torrentName, year, posterPath, recordRecent]. posterPath maybe stable per torrent. So effect re-runs only if those change.

Implement: 

```
const download = downloads.find(...);
const record = download && download.tmdbId && download.mediaType ? { tmdbId: download.tmdbId, mediaType: download.mediaType, title: download.title ?? download.torrentName, year: download.year, posterPath: download.posterPath } : null;
useEffect(() => {
  if (!record) return;
  recordRecent({ ...record, backdropPath: null, overview: '', voteAverage: 0 });
}, [record, recordRecent]);
```

`record` is new object every render → effect reruns every render again! no good.

Let me do primitive deps with a stable object memo:

Use useMemo over downloads snapshot to produce a primitive signature. Overkill.

Pragmatic senior decision: record only when there's no existing identical entry at front? Actually the simplest correct: depend on `download?.tmdbId` + record uses current store. Because WatchPage is unmounted when returning; if still mounted and store updates while the same movie, an extra record at front with same content is harmless — but still runs. Meh.

Given Watch page ephemeral and updates every 2s only while a movie is actually downloading/streaming; once seeding maybe no torrent update changes? The whole downloads array snapshot still replaced each poll even if equal values; store setDownloads sets new array each emit. So effect triggers every 2s regardless. To truly minimize, depend on primitive TMDB fields that stay constant, referencing them directly:

```
useEffect(() => {
  const d = downloads.find((x) => x.infoHash === infoHash.toLowerCase());
  if (!d || !d.tmdbId || !d.mediaType) return;
  recordRecent({ tmdbId: d.tmdbId, mediaType: d.mediaType, title: d.title ?? d.torrentName, year: d.year, posterPath: d.posterPath, ... });
  // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
}, [infoHash, recordRecent]);
```

With eslint-disable since deps intentionally coarse: effect runs when page/infoHash changes only. d read from current store each run. That's cleanest runtime-wise. We have lint enabled with react-hooks? Let me check eslint config to confirm rule presence & how disabling is treated (they have comment style seen? SearchPage had none). Let me just check config files.

Alternatively add a guard: only record if latest recents entry isn't already this same tmdbId viewed within current minute (avoid churn). Let me implement helper guard using store getState? recentsStore exposes no getState here; we can call record only if needed: In WatchPage use `const recents = useRecentsStore(s => s.recents)` and check `recents[0]?.item.tmdbId === tmdb && same type` and viewedAt within 5s → skip. That prevents poll churn elegantly without disable comment.

But detail page effect only reruns on id change anyway, fine.

For WatchPage implement guard effect with deps [infoHash, recordRecent, recents, download]? downloading each 2s changes download identity → effect rerun, guard checks recents front entry. recents stable unless changed → but we use recents variable in effect; add deps [infoHash, download?.infoHash, recents, recordRecent]? messy again but guard prevents setState (no-op skip). Using `recents` array new identity? recents only changes when we record (store updates). Between polls recents unchanged → effect sees same recents; run returns early no record → no churn. Works but reruns each poll scanning; trivial.

However effect deps referencing download object requires listing it. I'll include `download` in deps; since download object changes every 2s the effect reruns but guard prevents recording. Acceptable.

Guard logic:
```
useEffect(() => {
  if (!download || !download.tmdbId || !download.mediaType) return;
  const first = recents[0];
  if (first && first.item.tmdbId === download.tmdbId && first.item.mediaType === download.mediaType && Date.now() - first.viewedAt < 5000) return;
  recordRecent({...});
}, [download, recents, recordRecent]);
```

Wait recents dependency: after record, recents updates, effect reruns, guard passes (first is ours, recently) => no loop. good.

